e is whether digital pcr  could be a solution to address this  challenge  so before i explain how we have  addressed this question  i'll explain a bit how digital pcr works  in itself  so digital pcr for those of you who are  not familiar with the technology  is essentially the third generation of  pcr technologies  so the first pcr technology is endpoint  pcr  that is done in various indian labs and  was invented back in the 80s  and can be then analyzed using uh  electrophoresis cells so that's on the  left panel  it's a very useful technology but it's  not quantitative it's not very sensitive  at detecting dna or rna nucleic acids  then later on came the second generation  quantitative  dcr or real-time pcr and i guess the  most the best name for it is real-time  pcr  so what real-time pcr brought to the  table  is the ability to detect an increase in  the fluorescence  of of a sample as it is  it undergoes thermocycling and  dna replication and if you detect this  increasing fluorescence it means that  there is presence of the virus that  you're looking for in the sample  so this is a great technology that is  now the workhorse in all labs but it has  some issues that i just mentioned before  and particular sensitivity it's also  not an absolute quantity quantification  tool so meaning you don't really know  how many  material you started out with you just  you can't know whether or not you had  more or less to another reference sample  but it's not absolute  detection and quantification and so  later on came digital pcr and digital  pcr  third generation really addresses uh the  key issues of quantitative pcr  essentially it's an absolute  quantification tool so really counts  individual dna rna molecules one by one  you don't need any standard curve you're  really looking at individual molecules  and detecting those  reliably it provides increased  sensitivity  so you can detect those nucleic acids  with  single molecule sensitivity  and you have high precision you can  measure the quantity with uh  only a few percent of uncertainty so  it's obviously  a great um technology and  again focusing on the differences  between rt  qpcr and digital pcr the workflow would  essentially be the same  if you think again about the workflow i  presented above  you would still with digital pcr start  from an extracted sample so you would  collect  transport extract and instead of analyze  the sample  using rtqpcr you analyze the sample  using another instrument  which is performing digital pcr similar  chemistry similar assays  so from a workflow standpoint very  comparable so it can be easily  implemented  in labs that are already doing real-time  qpcr  but real-time qpcr as i said is mostly  for detection  of dna rrna  whereas digital pcr brings to the table  increased sensitivity absolute  quantification and also and that's i  think is interesting in the current  context of kubern-19 detection and  diagnostics it has low variability  between samples between um users and  between labs  and it's robust again against inhibitors  so it can essentially be implemented  in very similar ways anywhere you would  perform the assay giving similar results  across the globe which is interesting  when you want to compare performance of  assays here and there but what has to be  said again is that  right now our tpcr is the workhorse  technology  and it's the kuben 19 diagnostic  standard  and digital pcr still remains  a research tool and is only today  transitioning to diagnostics  and mostly has a purimetery tool for  kubern19 in particular  the hope we have at stila is that by  using digital pcr  we could indeed have a valuable valuable  asset  uh in the battle against cuban 19  by increasing sensitivity and by  increasing sensitivity basically we  could diagnose  patients who come in either late early  or late  into the testing facilities  if you see this middle graph here at the  moment real time pcr  is only able to diagnose patients who  h