re going to  matter  because diagnostics tests are able to  diagnose patients  who actively have the disease and  therefore diagnose  patients that present a risk to  contaminate  others and those those individuals  really need to be identified  and we need to take measures to make  sure that they don't infect  others serological tests are obviously  also important  but they only tell us who has been  infected before and who potentially has  immunity  but that will not allow us to prevent  the spread of the disease  further if we only do serological tests  so really the key in my opinion is going  to be  a diagnostics test and as of today  and it's most likely going to remain as  is  in the future um diagnostics tests are  mostly performed using  what is standardly called rt qpcr or  rt-pcr  so pcr with the retros transcript is  step done in real time and this rpt pcr  technology is now very common and used  worldwide to perform millions of tests  every day and again those tests really  are targeted at  patients and individuals who actively  have the disease  and those tests allow us to detect the  virus  from a patient sample  the workflow of rtbcr diagnostics  i believe is now common to to many  people around and probably everyone in  this audience but  it's art stuff with the sample  collection mostly done using  nasal swabs and then the the swab  is put into transport medium as a second  step and there is usually a transport  step to get the sample into the clinical  facility that will perform  the test to perform the test you have  two  main essential test steps sorry you have  the  extraction of the viral rna from the  transport medium  the extraction step and then you have  the analysis by a real-time pcr  so it's a four-step workflow uh  and there are out there solutions that  have integrated some of those steps  combining the extraction with the  analysis but in general this is the  standard workflow  which again uh is performed routinely in  millions of  of tests today but what we know as well  is that there are definitely some  limitations to rtq pcr  testings and there are multiple reports  where  those tests have not been able to  correctly diagnose patients  so one limitation that is known is  there have been frequent accounts of  patients having pneumonia  and having uh so ct abnormalities that  can be clearly seen on ct scans  so you can see such a ct scan on the  right  where you see in the lungs of the  patient's clear evidence  of liquid being present in the lungs  so the patients are obviously ct  positive but the  results of the real-time pcr tests has  come out negative  and that obviously is a  false negative test and is usually  confirmed either  by doing a test by rtpc or later on or  by doing  some sampling directly in the lung of  those patients and that has been  reported in  multiple journals today  similarly there are accounts saying that  real-time qpcr has its sensitivity  of 50 to 70 50 is probably a bit  pessimistic but definitely there is  a lack of sensitivity meaning that there  are about  30 percent of patients who have of  individuals who have the disease  who would not be detected by real-time  pcr  and if you go back to the workflow i  presented before  this could come from different um  origins one the sampling could not have  been performed correctly if you don't do  the same thing correctly if you don't  actually  use the swab correctly you're not going  to collect the virus and you're not  going to be able to detect it  also it's possible that the real-time  pcr analysis is not sensitive in enough  in itself so it's missing some virus  particles some virus genomes in the  sample that is being analyzed  and so in general there's clearly a need  to develop  more sensitive diagnostics workflow in  order to  address this challenge of being able to  grab to to reliably detect and diagnose  patients with cubit 19 and carrying  zarskov2 virus  and so at stila what we decided to  investigate is whether digital pcr  could be a solution to address this  challenge  so before i explain how we have  addressed this question  i'll explain a bit how digital pcr works  in itself  so digital pcr for those of you who are  not familiar with the technology  is essentially the third generation of  pcr technologies  so the first pcr technology is endpoint  pcr  that is done in various indian labs and  was invented back in the 80s  and can be then analyzed using uh  electrophoresis cells so that's on the  left panel  it's a very useful technology but it's  not quantitative it's not very sensitive  at detecting dna or rna nucleic acids  then later on came the second generation  quantitative  dcr or real-time pcr and i guess the  most the best name for it is real-time  pcr  so what real-time pcr brought to the  table  is the ability to detect an increase in  the fluorescence  of of a sample as it is  it undergoes thermocycling and  dna replication and if you detect this  increasing fluorescence it means that  there is presence of the virus that  you're looking for in the sample  so this is a great technology that is  now the workhorse in all labs but it has  some issues that i just mentioned before  and particular sensitivity it's also  not an absolute quantity quantification  tool so meaning you don't really know  how many  material you started out with you just  you can't know whether or not you had  more or less to another reference sample  but it's not absolute  detection and quantification and so  later on came digital pcr and digital  pcr  third generation really addresses uh the  key issues of quantitative pcr  essentially it's an absolute  quantification tool so really counts  individual dna rna molecules one by one  you don't need any standard curve you're  really looking at individual molecules  and detecting those  reliably it provides increased  sensitivity  so you can detect those nucleic acids  with  single molecule sensitivity  and you have high precision you can  measure the quantity with uh  only a few percent of uncertainty so  it's obviously  a great um technology and  again focusing on the differences  between rt  qpcr and digital pcr the workflow would  essentially be the same  if you think again about the workflow i  presented above  you would still with digital pcr start  from an extracted sample so you would  collect  transport extract and instead of analyze  the sample  using rtqpcr you analyze the sample  using another instrument  which is performing digital pcr similar  chemistry similar assays  so from a workflow standpoint very  comparable so it can be easily  implemented  in labs that are already doing real-time  qpcr  but real-time qpcr as i said is mostly  for detection  of dna rrna  whereas digital pcr brings to the table  increased sensitivity absolute  quantification and also and that's i  think is interesting in the current  context of kubern-19 detection and  diagnostics it has low variability  between samples between um users and  between labs  and it's robust again against inhibitors  so it can essentially be implemented  in very similar ways anywhere you would  perform the assay giving similar results  across the globe which is interesting  when you want to compare performance of  assays here and there but what has to be  said again is that  right now our tpcr is the workhorse  technology  and it's the kuben 19 diagnostic  standard  and digital pcr still remains  a research tool and is only today  transitioning to diagnostics  and mostly has a purimetery tool for  kubern19 in particular  the hope we have at stila is that by  using digital pcr  we could indeed have a valuable valuable  asset  uh in the battle against cuban 19  by increasing sensitivity and by  increasing sensitivity basically we  could diagnose  patients who come in either late early  or late  into the testing facilities  if you see this middle graph here at the  moment real time pcr  is only able to diagnose patients who  have  significant amounts of virus from the  samples collected  and this occurs you know at the peak of  of the infection  but we know that patients can already  transmit the disease early on  when they are asymptomatic and so it's  interesting to increase  the scope of diagnostics to the left of  this curve early on  disease and also what's happening  actually quite frequently  in in hospitals is that patients  unfortunately come in a bit late  already having severe symptoms but at a  point in the disease where it's not the  virus that's causing the symptoms  anymore it's the  immunoresponse of the patient that's  causing the symptoms  and in those cases again it's hard to  detect the virus from nasal swabs  using rt-pcr and so digital pcr could  also expand  the realm of diagnostics to the right of  this curve  and if we basically increase our ability  to diagnose patients we can really  understand better who's  has been infected by the disease and  who's not and then targets um  how we want to treat patients  accordingly  so how did pcr achieves higher  sensitivity higher precision  um it's by using a process  a workflow which uh amounts to  the following so you start off with your  your sample that you want to analyze  that contains  multiple amounts of dna  or rna molecules so various  amounts of dna molecules instead of  analyzing the sample  in bulk you start off by partitioning  the samples into  tens of thousands of subpartitions so  you cut  up your sample into micro volumes  and by doing so you isolate the dna  or rna molecules in different  compartments  so you initially have a complex sample  on the left  and then when you look at a single  partition it's a very simple  subsample where you only have one or two  dna molecules rna molecules  so you basically have simplified your  sample and then you're going to perform  pcr in all those partitions  but each pcr is going to be essentially  very keen because you're going to start  by your pcr reaction with a single  molecule and you don't have  cross reactions happening you don't have  noise in your pcr reactions and so you  get very clean  pcr amplifications and this pcr  amplification is then  combined with some some dyes that will  color  the droplets or the partitions that  contain the  dna or rna molecules of interest in some  color so blue in this case  so at the end of pcr you have a number  of partitions  which are colored and some who are not  colored  those who are colored become the  positive partitions those who are not  are the negatives  and then by just counting the number of  positives and negatives and applying  some statistics you can  go back and calculate very precisely the  amount of dna you had in your sample  uh so in this example you can get you  know only two or five percent precision  or uncertainty which is really  remarkable  but also think about the fact that if  you had a single partition becoming  positive this would mean that you had a  single  dna molecule in your sample and you  would really reliably detect this single  molecule  so you can have single molecule  sensitivity  so the workflow that i'll explain a bit  in more in detail is the  crystal crystal digital pcr workflow  that we have developed at stella  technologies  where we do this uh three-step workflow  partitioning pcr and analysis  in a format which we call droplet  crystals we partition the sample into  tens of thousands of  micro droplets and then those micro  droplets  self-assemble into a large 2d array that  we call a droplet crystal  and then we do the amplification step  and we do the readouts  using three fluorescence channels so we  do the readout using a blue  a green and red uh fluorescence  readout and this allows us to detect  three different  dna or rna targets and reliably quantify  those three different dna rna targets in  our sample  so this is our unique workflow to enable  multiplexing  precision and sensitivity in digital pcr  to perform the workflow we have  developed and we implement in the lab to  automate this workflow  we have our nike system so the nike  system  is built around a key consumable  our sapphire chip so this is a  microfluidic consumable  that really integrates and automates the  whole digital pcr workflow  and this is the key aspect in digital  pcr  how do you automate the partitioning  step the amplification step and the  readout  when you have to process tens of  thousands of partitions in parallel  so this automation is performed using  microfluidics and in our case using our  sapphire chip  so the sapphire chip is then processed  using two different instruments  nike geo than the prism 3 instrument  and at the end of the workflow also very  important  to enable our users and to enable  those who will be performing the test we  have our intuitive  crystal minor software that analyzes the  data  and gives results out to our users in  the numbers of  dna rna molecules present in their  sample  so our solution has three main  advantages when it comes to performing  high precision genetic analysis  it's easy to use as you'll see it's a  very simple process  and it's all integrated in a single  consumable  it's also a fast process it's the same  time to result as standard rt pcr  so about two hours and a half to get the  results out of the sapphire chips  and uh we also have this ability this  unique ability to detect  rna dna targets in three colors so you  can get more information out of our  out of your your samples  so as i said the heart of the product is  the sapphire chip so if you look at the  back side of the sapphire chip this is  where the automation takes place  it builds upon our proprietary  and patented partitioning technology  which allows us to take  the about you know 25 microliters of pcr  mix that is put in the chip and  transform that  into an array of 25 000 to 30 000  droplets  in each of the four chambers of the chip  so  each chip is capable of processing  fortune four samples in parallel  um and we can reliably make those uh  25 to 30 000 droppets uh in in our  samples using this microtic technology  and then process  the whole chip the whole samples in the  workflow in the chip  ease of use for us is key we have a  workflow which  can be performed by any lab technician  that is  accustomed to doing a pcr rt pcr  so to really prepare the chip you only  have to take the chip  remove the white caps uh and then  pipettes 25 micrometers of pcr mix  into the inlet ports the chemistries  that are being used are chemistries that  are  routinely found into in pcr labs so we  have a  specific master mix that needs to be  used that we provide  from quantum biosciences but then it's  compatible with  any type of probes tagman probes or even  evergreen intercalating dyes to perform  a variety of assays pcr assays that are  being done in labs that would adopt the  technology  so once you've loaded the chip you  transfer the chip into the geode  and the geode will inject the pcr mix  into the chip  and when it does the injection of the  pcr mix into the chip it  continuously breaks down the pcr mixing  those  droplet crystals of 30 000 droplets per  sample  and immediately after it performs the  pcr amplification  so the partitioning and amplification  tab steps take about two hours and  ten minutes and once this uh  step is performed the chips are strength  are  transferred to the second instruments to  the prism 3 instrument  which takes high-resolution images of  the droplet crystals  using three different fluorescence  channels so a blue channel  that can detect typically fan dyes a  green channel that can detect  uh big hex rocks size three dyes  so a broad range of the dyes can be used  for this channel and then a red  channel that can detect sci-fi or sci-fi  point  scythe 5.5 dies sorry  the readout is fast so it's less than a  minute per sample  um so basically in about two hours and  15 minutes you have the results  uh for your 12 samples that you would  have processed in the geode  and the last step that needs to be  automated is the image analysis and we  do so with our  crystal miner software which builds upon  ai tools to do image analysis and  extract the fluorescence  of all the droplets that you have in  your droplet crystals  so we process tens of thousands of  profits image droplets and we process  them through our  neural network software to make sure  that we can reliably detect those  droplets exclude artifacts measure the  fluorescence and basically  give out results which are one reliable  but also can be intuitively analyzed by  our users  to classify the droplets as positive for  the blue channel green channel  or red channel so it's really a powerful  software  which also provides you know quality  control trustworthy quality control  for example in terms of quality control  something that's quite unique  with the nike system is that you can  actually  look at the droplet crystals as the  images  of those droplet crystals and you can  overlay on top of the images  which droplets are positive for uh  one or the other of your your targets  and this is a reliable tool  for users who want to understand really  what's happening in their samples  and look at the quality of the results  that they're getting  so in summary with this  crystal digital pcr workflow and with  the nike system  we have enabled the automation of the  digital pcr workflow  in a single consumable uh with three  channel  fluorescence detection and that takes  the  in that takes place in about two hours  and 30 minutes with only about five  minutes of hands-on time  so really uh automated workflow that can  be simply implemented in the labs  and so this digital pcr workflow um  has already been implemented for a  variety of applications  um so the dominating applications i  would say prior to discover 19 epidemic  was oncology but you had applications  for environmental science for food  testing  for infectious disease any any  domain of science where you need to do  genetics molecular testing has  potential uses for digital pcr and  digital pcr is really becoming the new  standard  for precision genetic analysis  for those who are interested in learning  more about digital pcr  in itself i can point you to a website  called gene pi  which we have built together with some  collaborators  as really an educational portal for  people who want to learn how digital pcr  performs  learn about the math behind the  statistics and how it can be applied  to different cases like rare event  mutation detections  like copy number variation  quantification or  to perform more complex assays like  drop-off assays  so really good tool to learn more about  digital pcr in general  not just on the nike system but in  general how digital pcr can be useful  for genetics  so let me now turn to how digital pcr  again can be used  in the context of qubit 19 and  our battle against the epidemic so as i  said  something that we investigated  at stila is whether or not digital pcr  could be used to increase sensitivity  and reduce  false negative rates that have been  observed by real-time pcr  so to do so um the first thing that we  need  is an assay that can be used to detect  the sarso  virus and so we have developed  an assay to do exactly that with our  partners  apex bio who are based in suzhou in  china  so we have worked with ethics bio suju  very early on in the outbreak of the  epidemic we were  lucky in contact with our partners there  who were you know  first to witness the outbreak and so as  of  starting even in january this we started  working on the development of this assay  which is uh the currently the only  uh threeplex assay for the detection of  starscope 2. so the assay is set up as  following  so we have detection on the fan channel  of the orf-1ab gene of the source code 2  virus  on the green channel we detect the  nucleoprotein n  of the viral genome and in the  cy5 channel we have a human house group  keeping gene that's being detected as an  internal  endogenous control which is also very  important to make sure  that when you get a negative result it's  a true negative and not  a malfunction of the instrument of the  essay itself  so this assay is available to any user  uh  who wants to implement the nike system  in digital pcr  to source for source code to detection  and it's available worldwide  before i go into the presentation of  some of the results we've obtained with  this  essay i'll talk a bit about  the design of the assay itself and  how you can use the multiplexing  capability  of the the nike system to have increased  sensitivity  and so here what's shown is a picture  of the structure of the viral genome so  what's interesting with source code 2 is  you know it's a very long  genome for our virus it's almost 30 kb  long  and so we have chosen to  have two different targets in our assay  that are spread apart  one from another into genome so one of  our targets is  rf1abg so it's located to the left of  the genome  and the other is on the n gene so those  two targets are separated from one  another  with a distance of you know more than 10  000  bases base pairs so they are very spread  apart  and what's interesting here is that in  practice  viral rna once it's analyzed  in digital pcr or real-time pcr  the viral rna is quite highly fragmented  this fragmentation of the genome occurs  probably already at collection it most  likely occurs during transport where  the virus itself is sliced and then  it also occurs quite extensively in the  extraction process  so when you analyze the virus itself you  don't analyze 30 kb  fragments you only have fragments of a  few hundred kbs  and so one genome actually  becomes two different fragments at the  very least detected in our system  you get you if you have one genome in  your sample you would get one green  droplet and you would get  one red droplet so you get two events  per