g how digital pcr can be used as a  complementary tool for uh diagnostics of  critical patients  as those  a similar study that can be uh  shared today is one that we performed at  the yashu marseille again it's a  hospital  in france and there  the goal of the study was to look at  qpcr results which are  basically dubious doubtful so when you  have a  qpcr results you typically use look at  the ct value that comes out so at which  cycle  do you get a fluorescence readout for  your sample and typically when the  patient when the sample has a lot of  virus the ct value is low you  find out fluorescence values early on in  the amplification step so the ct is  below 35  and uh but when you have low amount of  virus  you can have amplification at very late  stages when the ct is then above 35  but it's unclear if  when you have a city that's above 35  whether this is a true positive  results or if it's positive because of  of some polymers error or some  amplification that's not specific to the  source co2 virus  so we  we looked at uh at a set of samples  uh we looked at 15 samples that were  negative by qpcr  12 that were clearly positive with city  values below 35  and 18 with who had doubtful results  first interesting result is that amongst  the negative we had one recall  a sample that was clearly positive by  digital pcr  again it's likely to be due to the  increased sensitivity of digital pcr  compared  to real-time pcr  out of the 12 positive samples we could  see that we could reliably detect  the virus by a digital pcr and also we  found  a good correlation between the ct value  and the quantity of iris that's detected  by digital pcr the lower  the ct value the higher the  concentration measured by digital pcr  the correlation is not full because here  the qpcr  assay is not standardized  and so it's hard to really say and  compare qpcr results one to another  something that i pointed out to before  without any standard curve you can't  really compare patient a to patient b  which could have been processed  differently which could have different  contents of inhibitors etc  but at least there is a good correlation  as expected now looking at the 18  doubtful results in in qpcr  those had ct values that ranged  from 34 to about 40  in terms of ct values and we processed  them by digital pcr  and the key results is that we actually  reliably detected  the viral the virus in 11 out of those  18 samples  seven only out of 18 came out negative  and so here again we have more than half  of those  doubtful results which become clearly  positive  by digital pcr and we could then use  this confirmatory tool  in terms of patient management in  hospitals to make sure that patients who  have ict values  but for digital pcr positive can be put  in kubernetes  facilities and hospitals and those who  are negative on both  can be treated separately with less risk  of  infecting others so those are only two  studies  preliminary at this point in time  but who interestingly point out clearly  advantages of using digital pcr compared  to qpcr  what has to be said is that this  approach  is now routinely used in china um  in in the center for disease control  which are using a digital pcr to confirm  doubtful results  on several hundreds of samples and this  is something that we hope we can also  uh implement uh in in europe and in the  us  with with us with our approach  so this was an illustration of how  digital pcr can be used to cover 19  detection and quantification but digital  pcr as i said can be used for  a number of other challenges  in the realm of genetic analysis and  molecular diagnostics  so we have a number of application notes  that can be found on our website  and those results and this approach can  also be uh  found in the publication we have on our  website  specifically dedicated to kubern 19  detection  [Music]  so with this i conclude my presentation  so i really want to thank the  the doctors and physicians who 